Of course! Here is an example of how to create a console application in .NET using C# that displays a progress bar:
usingSystem;

namespace ProgressBarConsoleApp
{
    classProgram
    {
        static void Main(string[] args)
        {
            // We define the number of iterations we want for our progress bar
            int numIterations = 100;

            // We configure the console to show the progress bar
            Console.CursorVisible = false;

            // We iterate through each iteration of the progress bar
            for (int i = 0; i <= numIterations; i++)
            {
                // We calculate the percentage completed
                double percentComplete = (double)i / numIterations;

                // We calculate the width of the progress bar (in characters)
                int barWidth = Console.WindowWidth - 1;

                // We calculate the number of characters that will be displayed in the progress bar
                int charsToShow = (int)Math.Floor(percentComplete * barWidth);

                // We create the string that represents the progress bar
                string progressBar = "[" + new string('=', charsToShow) + new string(' ', barWidth - charsToShow) + "]";

                // Print the progress bar in the console
                Console.SetCursorPosition(0, Console.CursorTop);
                Console.Write(progressBar);

                // We wait a brief moment to pretend that some work is being done
                System.Threading.Thread.Sleep(50);
            }

            // We print a line break so that the progress bar does not take up the entire console
            Console.WriteLine();
            Console.WriteLine("Process completed. Press any key to exit.");
            Console.ReadKey();
        }
    }
}


This example uses a for loop to iterate through each iteration of the progress bar. In each iteration, the percentage complete is calculated, the width of the progress bar is calculated, the number of characters to display in the progress bar is calculated, and the string representing the progress bar is created. The progress bar is then printed to the console and you wait a short moment before continuing to the next iteration.

